            "This is where lawyers are different from most businesses. A restaurant can explain exactly what went wrong with an order. A lawyer usually cannot, because confidentiality rules protect information about a representation, and simply confirming that someone was a client can reveal more than you should. Several state bars have disciplined lawyers who shared client details while replying to bad reviews.",
            "A safe reply is short and general. Thank the person for taking the time to share feedback, say that your firm takes all concerns seriously, explain that professional rules limit what you can say about any matter, and invite them to contact the office directly. Do not argue, do not correct details and do not hint at what happened.",
            "Wait before replying. A reply written while angry almost always reads badly. Give it a day, have someone else read it, and keep in mind that your reply is written for future clients who will read it, not for the reviewer.",
